      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to understand how the train's speed affects both the time it takes for the train to clear the tunnel and how the length of the tunnel can be calculated once we know the lengths of the train and the tunnel, and the time it takes for the train to clear them.\n\n1. **Convert the train's speed from miles per hour to miles per minute**:\n   Given that the train is moving at 60 miles per hour, we can convert this speed into miles per minute (mph) by dividing by 60 (since there are 60 minutes in an hour). This conversion gives us \\(60\\, \\text{mp/h} ÷ 60\\, \\text{mp}\\) = 1\\, \\text{mp/min}\\). Here, 1\\, \\text{mp/min} is used to convert the distance calculation from miles to minutes.\n\n2. **Calculate the time the train spends clearing the tunnel**:\n   We are told that the front of the train exits the tunnel exactly 3 minutes after it enters the tunnel. This time corresponds to the train's movement through the length of the tunnel.\n\n3. **Calculate the length of the tunnel**:\n   Since the train's speed is 1\\, \\text{mp/min} and it takes this speed of movement to travel through the tunnel for 3 minutes, the total distance covered by the train during this time corresponds to the length of the tunnel.\n   \n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{cc}\n   train \\ text{ speed \\ (mp/min)} & \\text{distance (mp)} \\\\\n   1 & \\text{time \\ (minutes)}\\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n   Thus, the length of the tunnel = 3 minutes x 1\\, \\text{mp/min} = 3\\, \\text{mp}\n   \n   Therefore, the length of the tunnel is 3 miles.\n\nConclusion:\nBased on the calculations above, the length of the tunnel the train passes through is \\(\\boxed{3}\\) miles.",
